[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Contract Inheritance and MRO (C3 Linearization) in Solidity"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Solidity supports multiple inheritance, allowing a smart contract to inherit functions and state variables from multiple base contracts. When inheritance gets complex, Solidity uses the C3 Linearization algorithm to determine the Method Resolution Order (MRO). Understanding MRO is crucial for avoiding compilation errors and managing function overriding correctly."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"How Solidity Handles Inheritance"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"When you inherit from multiple contracts, you must specify them in a specific order from 'most base-like' to 'most derived'. If you fail to order them correctly based on their dependency graph, the Solidity compiler will throw a linearization error."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
pragma solidity ^0.8.0;

contract A {
    function foo() public pure virtual returns (string memory) {
        return "A";
    }
}

contract B is A {
    function foo() public pure virtual override returns (string memory) {
        return "B";
    }
}

contract C is A {
    function foo() public pure virtual override returns (string memory) {
        return "C";
    }
}

// Correct ordering based on C3 linearization
contract D is B, C {
    function foo() public pure override(B, C) returns (string memory) {
        return super.foo();
    }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Rules of C3 Linearization"},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Monotonicity: The derived contract's order must preserve the ordering of its base contracts."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"First-tail priority: When merging lists, the head of the list is selected if it does not appear in the tail of any other list."},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the example above, contract D inherits from B and C. Because C overrides A and B overrides A, the compiler needs a definitive path to resolve super.foo(). C3 linearization ensures that D evaluates C first, then B, then A (right-to-left in base declaration, evaluated left-to-right in execution context depending on the graph)."},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Using Super and Overriding Multiple Contracts"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"When a function is defined in multiple inherited contracts, the derived contract must explicitly override all conflicting versions. The keyword 'override(Base1, Base2)' is mandatory to resolve ambiguity."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"contract FinalContract is B, C {
    function foo() public pure override(B, C) returns (string memory) {
        return super.foo();
    }
}"},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always declare base contracts from the most base-like to the most derived."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use the virtual keyword in base contract functions that are meant to be overridden."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use the override keyword in child contracts, and specify parents explicitly if there is diamond inheritance."}]
